\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{From Frobenius-Schur indicators to real Gaussian types}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{W} be an irreducible real representation of a finite group,
#{D=\operatorname{End}_G(W)}, and #{\chi} an irreducible constituent
of #{W_{\mathbb C}=W\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C}.
The [Frobenius-Schur indicator](fgap-0021) gives exactly the following
possibilities, where #{X_\chi} denotes a complex module of character
#{\chi}:
##{\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\nu_2(\chi)&D&W_{\mathbb C}&\dim_{\mathbb R}W\\\hline
1&\mathbb R&X_\chi&\chi(1)\\
0&\mathbb C&X_\chi\oplus X_{\bar\chi}&2\chi(1)\\
-1&\mathbb H&X_\chi\oplus X_\chi&2\chi(1).
\end{array}}
In the middle row #{\chi\ne\bar\chi}. In the last row #{\chi(1)}
is even and #{\dim_{\mathbb H}W=\chi(1)/2}.
The form trichotomy alone is not the real descent argument;
these assertions use the bridge proved below. They agree with
\citet{Section 13.2, Proposition 39, p. 109}{serre1977linear}.}

\proof{
\p{Choose a real basis of #{W}. Commutation with the matrices of the
group operators in that basis is a system of real linear equations.
Complexifying its solution space
therefore gives
##{\operatorname{End}_G(W_{\mathbb C})
 \cong D\otimes_{\mathbb R}\mathbb C.}
For #{D=\mathbb R,\mathbb C,\mathbb H} the right side is respectively
#{\mathbb C}, #{\mathbb C\times\mathbb C}, #{M_2(\mathbb C)}.
For the middle identity use
#{z\otimes a\mapsto(za,\bar z a)}; for the last use the quaternion model of \ref{fcap-0042}, the usual
inclusion of #{\mathbb H} into #{M_2(\mathbb C)}, whose complex span
is all of #{M_2(\mathbb C)}.
Complex semisimplicity identifies the left side with
#{\prod_\psi M_{n_\psi}(\mathbb C)}, where #{n_\psi} is the
constituent multiplicity. Its simple factors and their dimensions
force one constituent once, two distinct constituents once each,
or one constituent twice, respectively.}

\p{Let #{c(w+iv)=w-iv}. It exchanges the #{\psi} and
#{\bar\psi} components. If #{D=\mathbb R}, the single complex
irreducible has the real form #{W}. Averaging a real inner product
and complexifying it supplies an invariant nondegenerate symmetric
bilinear form, so #{\nu_2=1}. If #{D=\mathbb C} and either of the
two inequivalent components were fixed by #{c}, its fixed vectors
would be a nonzero proper real invariant subspace of #{W}.
Indeed, for a #{c}-stable space every vector is uniquely
#{(x+cx)/2+i(x-cx)/(2i)} with both components fixed by #{c}.
Irreducibility excludes this, so the two characters are a nonreal
conjugate pair and #{\nu_2=0}.}

\p{If #{D=\mathbb H}, conjugation fixes the sole constituent type,
so #{\chi=\bar\chi} and #{\nu_2=\pm1}. Suppose #{\nu_2=1}.
Choose a nondegenerate invariant symmetric complex bilinear form
#{B} on #{X_\chi} and an averaged invariant positive Hermitian
form #{h}, conjugate-linear in its first argument. There is a unique
antilinear map #{J} on the complex irreducible #{X_\chi}, not on
the real module #{W}, with #{B(x,y)=h(Jx,y)}, and it commutes with
#{G}. In an #{h}-orthonormal basis of #{X_\chi} write
#{B(x,y)=x^TAy}.
Then #{A^T=A}, #{Jx=\bar A\bar x} and
#{J^2=\bar A A=A^{\dagger}A>0}. By complex Schur's lemma
#{J^2=t\bbI} for a positive real #{t}. Replacing #{J} by
#{t^{-1/2}J} makes #{J^2=\bbI}. Its fixed space #{U} is a real
form of #{X_\chi}, by the same two-component formula. This is the
irreducible specialization of
\citet{Section 13.2, Theorem 31, pp. 106-108}{serre1977linear}.}

\p{The real module #{U} is irreducible, since a proper real
invariant subspace would complexify to a proper subspace of
#{X_\chi}. There is a nonzero complex intertwiner
#{U_{\mathbb C}\to W_{\mathbb C}=X_\chi^{\oplus2}}.
Choose real bases of #{U} and #{W}. In those bases, its real or
imaginary part gives a nonzero real intertwiner #{U\to W}, which
must be an isomorphism by irreducibility. But #{\dim_{\mathbb R}U=\chi(1)} and
#{\dim_{\mathbb R}W=2\chi(1)}, a contradiction.
Thus #{\nu_2=-1}. The three division algebras exhaust the cases
and give distinct indicators, so all implications reverse.
Finally #{W} is a left #{\mathbb H}-space, making its real dimension
a multiple of 4; hence #{\chi(1)} is even.}

\p{In the scalar-column coordinates #{z_1,\ldots,z_r\in D^m} of
\ref{fdip-0050}, the real density is proportional to
##{\exp\left(-\frac12\sum_{a=1}^r
\operatorname{Re}(z_a^{\dagger}Q_\Xi z_a)\right),}
and the real covariance is #{Q_\Xi^{-1}} on each column. For
#{D=\mathbb C}, multiplication of a column by #{\ue{\ui\theta}} preserves
this real law. With that real normalization the complex second moment
is #{\mathbb E[z_az_a^{\dagger}]=2Q_\Xi^{-1}}. For #{D=\mathbb H}, right
multiplication #{z_a\mapsto z_aq} by a unit quaternion preserves
#{\operatorname{Re}(z^{\dagger}Q_\Xi z)} and Lebesgue measure. Left
multiplication by an arbitrary quaternion need not commute with the
entries of #{Q_\Xi}. Neither symmetry is an additional graph symmetry.
The [coordinate reading](fdip-00KQ) records these formulas. The real
Gaussian normalization is that of \ref{fdip-000H}.}
}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{Representation.frobeniusSchurIndicator_eq_one_iff_isRealizableOverReal}
proves the indicator-one/real-realizability equivalence for an
irreducible complex representation of a finite group. It supports
the real-form step; the full commutant identification, all three
real dimensions and the Gaussian coordinate statements are the
arguments above.}
